In Django you can define a **custom model manager** to encapsulate a reusable query, such as “give me only the posts that are published” [4].

Why it works: every model already has a default manager called **objects** that returns all rows [3][2]. By subclassing `models.Manager` and overriding its `get_queryset()` method you can change the initial `QuerySet` that the manager produces; the overridden method can filter on any field, for example the `status` field to keep only `PUBLISHED` rows [4][1]. If you add a custom manager and still want the original `objects` manager, you must declare it explicitly; otherwise Django replaces the default manager with only your custom one [1].

Worked example (from the textbook):

```python
class PublishedManager(models.Manager):
    def get_queryset(self):
        return super().get_queryset().filter(status=Post.Status.PUBLISHED)

class Post(models.Model):
    objects = models.Manager()          # keep the default manager
    published = PublishedManager()      # our custom manager
```

Now you can retrieve all published posts with `Post.published.all()`, and further filter, e.g. `Post.published.filter(title__startswith='Who')` as shown in the shell example [1].

Common mistake: forgetting to re‑add the default `objects` manager when you declare a custom one, which silently removes the ability to call `Post.objects.all()` and can break code that expects the default manager [1].
